WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:05.720
This video was made possible by the Crew 2—a
new open world racing game from Ubisoft.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:08.200
More about it after the video.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.879
No sport is as logistically challenging as
motorsports.

00:00:11.879 --> 00:00:17.830
While equipment matters in any sport, in motorsports
the human is only half the athlete—the vehicle

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:19.090
is the other half.

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:23.529
The performance of a motorsports vehicle is
directly tied to having the right components

00:00:23.529 --> 00:00:28.269
in the right place at the right time so the
logistics are part of the competition.

00:00:28.269 --> 00:00:31.710
Racing may be a sport but it’s not all fun
and games.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:35.860
Teams are businesses—businesses that are
expected to make money.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:42.180
The most valuable Formula 1 team—Scuderia
Ferrari—is worth over $1.3 billion.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:46.780
This means this team is valued at about as
much as tech companies Discord, Bird Rides,

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:47.900
or DoorDash.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.850
The wealthier teams, like Ferrari, are able
to spend more money on transport to bring

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:55.600
more equipment and spare parts which can make
the difference between winning and losing

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.290
when things go wrong.

00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:01.960
Formula 1 is by many measures the most international
sports competition.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:07.260
Over its 21 yearly races the teams traverse
five continents with as little as a week between

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:08.260
events.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:13.170
A chaotic ballet of trucks, boats, and planes
transports this show across the world each

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:14.170
year.

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:18.990
Ten teams compete in Formula 1 and despite
it taking place worldwide it is by all measures

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:20.620
a European sport.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:24.159
Eight of the ten teams are registered in and
operate out of Europe.

00:01:24.159 --> 00:01:28.370
Only the Indian and American teams are from
elsewhere although, the Indian team is actually

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:33.470
based in the UK while the US team, based in
North Carolina, operates a secondary forward

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:37.400
base in the UK so that its staff doesn’t
have to travel all the way back to the US

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:41.560
between each of the European races which in
recent years have been held consecutively,

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:45.250
with the brief interruption of the Canadian
Grand Prix, in the middle of the season over

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:46.250
summer.

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:50.080
Thanks to this, the European leg of the season
is, compared to the rest, relatively easy

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:52.840
logistically because within Europe you can
drive.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:57.850
The cost of shipping by truck is so comparatively
low to shipping by plane that teams bring

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:00.810
whole buildings with them to the European
races.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:04.540
These buildings are what are modestly referred
to as “motorhomes” but can be as large

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:09.079
as Red Bull’s three story structure that
includes offices, bars, and a restaurant with

00:02:09.079 --> 00:02:10.439
a complete kitchen.

00:02:10.439 --> 00:02:15.430
All of that packs into a number of trucks
and can be assembled in less than two days.

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:19.939
Along with all the other equipment including
cars, spare parts, and electronics, convoys

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:24.689
of dozens of trucks per team criss cross the
continent before each European race.

00:02:24.689 --> 00:02:28.639
Typically races are held every two weekends
on Sunday which gives teams plenty of time

00:02:28.639 --> 00:02:32.750
to relocate before activity begins on the
Thursday before the Grand Prix but from time

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:36.870
to time the schedule is crunched and races
take place two weekends in a row.

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:41.359
This proves a more daunting logistical challenge
as teams only have three full days to break

00:02:41.359 --> 00:02:45.980
down, transport, and reassemble their equipment
at the new race site.

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:51.189
Even worse, for the first time ever the 2018
season saw three weekends with three races

00:02:51.189 --> 00:02:52.189
in a row.

00:02:52.189 --> 00:02:56.209
On June 24 the French Grand Prix took place
in Le Castellet then the next weekend the

00:02:56.209 --> 00:02:59.819
Austrian one was held in Spielberg finishing
off the following weekend with the British

00:02:59.819 --> 00:03:01.609
Grand Prix in Silverstone.

00:03:01.609 --> 00:03:06.989
The Austria-UK transfer was the most difficult
one as it involved driving nearly 1,000 miles

00:03:06.989 --> 00:03:09.919
including through the choke-point of the channel
tunnel.

00:03:09.919 --> 00:03:14.139
For this trek, each truck was manned by three
drivers so that while one drove the others

00:03:14.139 --> 00:03:16.610
could sleep in an RV that accompanied the
convoy.

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:20.999
That way, the trucks could drive continuously
only stopping to refuel.

00:03:20.999 --> 00:03:26.090
But again, these races are easy logistically
compared to the ones outside of Europe—the

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:28.809
ones known as flyaway races.

00:03:28.809 --> 00:03:32.739
Just like with the European races the majority
of flyaway races occur with two weeks between

00:03:32.739 --> 00:03:37.510
them but from time to time races are scheduled
on back to back weekends thousands of miles

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:38.530
apart.

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:43.329
These back to back flyaway races are the most
logistically difficult weeks of the Formula

00:03:43.329 --> 00:03:44.449
1 season.

00:03:44.449 --> 00:03:49.209
On Sunday April 8th, 2018 the Bahrain Grand
Prix was held in Sakhir, Bahrain then seven

00:03:49.209 --> 00:03:53.840
days later, the following weekend, the Chinese
Grand Prix was held in Shanghai, China.

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:58.999
Over 4,000 miles separated those two racetracks
and yet, just like with every race, everything

00:03:58.999 --> 00:04:04.629
came down on Sunday night in Bahrain and had
to be operational by Thursday morning in China.

00:04:04.629 --> 00:04:09.459
Even worse, Shanghai is five hours ahead of
Bahrain which is effectively five hours fewer

00:04:09.459 --> 00:04:14.989
to do the work but in reality, the planning
for this transfer began months before.

00:04:14.989 --> 00:04:19.109
Around January 2018, three months before the
first races of the season, each of the ten

00:04:19.109 --> 00:04:22.300
teams packed up five sets of shipping containers.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:27.380
Each of these sets held their sea kits carrying
things like chairs, tables, appliances, cooking

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:29.699
utensils, and some elements of their garages.

00:04:29.699 --> 00:04:34.110
They send these bulkier and less expensive
pieces of equipment by sea as it’s massively

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:36.780
less expensive than sending them by plane.

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:40.310
The number of containers per team varies as
the wealthier teams like Red Bull will take

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:44.750
more but in general each team takes about
three 40 foot containers.

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:48.979
Of course ocean shipping is slower, but since
there are five sets there is always one at

00:04:48.979 --> 00:04:51.419
the right place at the right time.

00:04:51.419 --> 00:04:55.570
That January shipment sent the first five
kits to the first five flyaway races—Melbourne,

00:04:55.570 --> 00:05:01.349
Australia; Sakhir, Bahrain; Shanghai, China;
Baku, Azerbaijan; and Montreal, Canada.

00:05:01.349 --> 00:05:05.650
Then, as each race was completed its kit was
packed up and sent to the next flyaway race

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:10.060
destination without a kit—the Australian
one went to Singapore, the Bahraini one to

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:14.960
Russia, the Chinese one to Japan, the Azerbaijani
one to the United States, and the Canadian

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:18.460
one to Mexico, and then towards the end of
the season, when there are no more tracks

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:22.030
to send kits to, they’re sent back to the
teams’ home bases for the winter.

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:26.340
At the racetrack, the Formula 1 logistics
team’s main downtime is actually during

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:31.210
the race itself but for the Bahrain to China
transfer real work began the Thursday before

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:32.210
the race.

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:36.310
That’s when each team’s logistics manager
started making their tear-down plan—deciding

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:40.460
in which order and into which containers their
different pieces of equipment should go.

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:44.520
Once that was completed there was really not
a lot to do until Sunday.

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:48.580
On Sunday morning, before the race even started,
the pack up began.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:51.630
Many of the spare parts can’t be used during
the race—they’re not going to replace

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:55.550
an engine during the Grand Prix—so they’re
the first pieces of kit to be packed into

00:05:55.550 --> 00:05:56.830
their containers.

00:05:56.830 --> 00:06:00.979
Not much happened during the race itself but
within 15 minutes of it ending the main pack

00:06:00.979 --> 00:06:02.090
up began.

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:06.410
The cars, the most important pieces of equipment,
were subjected to a post-race inspection to

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:10.800
be sure no illegal modifications were made
but everything else was immediately ready

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:11.800
for packing.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:15.889
All the equipment that the teams wanted at
the destination first were put in one of three

00:06:15.889 --> 00:06:17.669
priority pallets.

00:06:17.669 --> 00:06:21.830
Together each team’s priority pallets filled
up the first plane to Shanghai.

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:25.819
As soon as these were packed they were driven
directly to the airport only a few hours after

00:06:25.819 --> 00:06:29.430
the race ended to be prepared for an early
morning flight to Shanghai.

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:33.669
As that plane took off the final pallets were
being packed up back at the racetrack.

00:06:33.669 --> 00:06:37.960
Only about six to eight hours after the drop
of the checkered flag, all pallets were packed

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:40.300
up and on their way to the airport.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:45.680
By midday Monday, all six of the Boeing 747’s
used to transport the teams equipment to China

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:46.680
were in the air.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:51.340
These planes were chartered by Formula 1 but
the teams still pay for the space.

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:54.970
Also on Monday all the staff started their
journey to Shanghai.

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:58.890
Many of the lower level staff just flew on
normal commercial flights while some of the

00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:02.800
higher profile drivers flew private between
the two countries.

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:06.970
After nine hours in the air the first plane
touched down in Shanghai around early evening

00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:08.110
local time.

00:07:08.110 --> 00:07:10.490
The freight was unloaded and brought to customs.

00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:14.400
By midnight, all the priority freight was
on its way to the racetrack.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.370
Overnight, logistics workers arranged the
different teams’ freight into their respective

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:19.370
paddocks.

00:07:19.370 --> 00:07:22.969
No team is allowed to touch their freight
until all the other teams’ freight has arrived

00:07:22.969 --> 00:07:27.050
both for fairness purposes and safety to assure
that there aren’t many people around as

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:29.340
pallets are being unloaded and moved.

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:32.509
By Tuesday morning, it’s game on for the
assembly crews.

00:07:32.509 --> 00:07:36.469
At this point each team had their three priority
pallets and their sea kit.

00:07:36.469 --> 00:07:40.590
In their priority pallets teams didn’t put
the highest value or most important cargo,

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:44.999
they put the things they needed to assemble
first—the bare bones of their garage.

00:07:44.999 --> 00:07:48.639
That includes the wall paneling, the core
of their electrical system, and the majority

00:07:48.639 --> 00:07:50.999
of the IT and communications equipment.

00:07:50.999 --> 00:07:54.729
By Tuesday evening that build was done and
the track was once again made inaccessible

00:07:54.729 --> 00:07:58.569
to the teams as the non-priority pallets were
delivered overnight.

00:07:58.569 --> 00:08:02.870
Early Wednesday morning around 6am the teams
arrived back at the track and began work on

00:08:02.870 --> 00:08:05.010
the final assembly of the garage.

00:08:05.010 --> 00:08:09.849
Only after about four hours, by late morning,
all the different teams garages were operational.

00:08:09.849 --> 00:08:15.020
All in all, ten Formula 1 teams successfully
packed up, shipped all their equipment 4,000

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:18.720
miles, and reassembled their paddocks in 58
hours.

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:24.499
Thanks to careful planning and practiced workers,
Formula 1 pulls off this impressive feat every

00:08:24.499 --> 00:08:26.939
year without a hitch.

00:08:26.939 --> 00:08:31.569
Earlier in the video I mentioned how the short
7-day Spielberg, Austria to Silverstone, UK

00:08:31.569 --> 00:08:36.200
transfer is the hardest race transfer in Europe
but interestingly, about a month ago Ubisoft

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:40.460
brought me out to the Spielberg Formula 1
track to try out their new racing game—the

00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:41.460
Crew 2.

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:46.150
This game is open world meaning they actually
recreated the entire US and you can drive

00:08:46.150 --> 00:08:51.720
and race through all of it not only in cars
but also motorcycles, boats, and planes.

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:56.150
Even as someone who doesn’t play video games
much, I enjoyed the Crew 2 a lot and it seemed

00:08:56.150 --> 00:09:00.880
an accurate recreation of the actual racing
I did that day on the Spielberg track.

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:04.590
If you think you might be interested in playing
this, there will be a link in the description

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:06.090
where you can check it out.

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:10.890
Thanks to the support of the Crew 2 and Ubisoft,
this video is an extra one so there will be

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:12.950
another Wendover video out next week.

